{"qid":"Q1036","names":["Republic of Uganda"],"title":"Republic of Uganda","endonym":{"native":"Republic of Uganda","lang":"en"},"capsule":"Uganda became an independent state on 9 October 1962, initially as a constitutional monarchy within the Commonwealth, and was declared a republic in 1963 with Kabaka Mutesa II as the first president. The post-independence decades were marked by Idi Amin's military dictatorship (1971–1979), during which an estimated 100,000–500,000 Ugandans were killed, and by the subsequent Uganda–Tanzania War that ended his rule. Since Yoweri Museveni seized power in 1986, Uganda has maintained relative internal stability, developing a diverse economy centred on coffee, tourism, and oil, though facing ongoing security challenges in its northern and eastern borderlands.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Boundaries as a modern state are precisely defined by colonial treaties and United Nations records; internal administrative boundaries are well documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Uganda","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Uganda"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Uganda","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uganda"},{"label":"Britannica: Idi Amin","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Idi-Amin"}],"britannica":"place/Uganda","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Uganda","rulers":[{"qid":"Q356026","name":"Muteesa II of Buganda","start":1963,"end":1966,"image":"Sir. Edward Fredrick Mutesa, The President of Uganda.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Sir.%20Edward%20Fredrick%20Mutesa%2C%20The%20President%20of%20Uganda.jpg?width=320","imageLice